  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1039 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA 599 dam ridge remains centered over Arkansas as of Thursday\nevening. This ridge will continue to slowly inch southwestward as\nbroad troughing emerges over the Pacific Northwest. Due to the\nintensity of the ridge, the area wide Heat Advisory remains in\neffect through 8 PM Saturday. The trough will gradually impinge\non the ridge projecting heights to fall to around 590 dam by\nSunday morning. Uncertainty remains regarding whether the Heat\nAdvisory may need to be extended into Sunday. Dewpoints are\ntrending towards the low to mid 60s, but air temperatures will\ncontinue to be in the upper 90s to lower 100s. For now, we will\nmonitor these trends and the evolution of height falls.\n\nA subtle synoptic change is favored for early next week as the\naforementioned trough targets a weak cold front at the Mid-South\non Monday. This will bring isolated to scattered PoPs into the\nforecast during the afternoon and evening, but appreciable\nrainfall is not anticipated with this activity. Probabilities of\nreceiving 0.25\" of rainfall are less than 20%. Subtle ridging\nwill build in quickly behind the boundary, but so far guidance\ndoes not depict an anomalous ridge as we are currently\nexperiencing.\n\nWednesday could be the first hint at 'relief', though still 8-\n10 degrees above normal for the end of September. NBM percentiles\nhave a large spread, 9-12 degree differences between the 25th and\n75th percentile; so most of the guidance is favoring a slight\ncooldown. This seems to be due to troughing being projected set\nup across the eastern CONUS. For now, continue to practice heat\nsafety and follow any local burn bans.\n\nDNM",
